<section class="farmer-card"><h2>What the inputs suggest</h2><p>This is a pre-sowing planning assessment. The crop is not yet standing, so the photograph cannot establish a wheat variety, crop age, disease or yield.</p><p>The illustrative pH, nutrient and conductivity values are inputs to discuss with a soil-testing laboratory or local agricultural adviser. Their interpretation depends on the laboratory method, soil type, sampling depth and local recommendations. They do not justify a fertiliser dose on their own.</p><p>The borewell entry describes a possible irrigation source. It does not establish reliable water supply, pumping capacity or groundwater suitability.</p></section>
<section class="farmer-card"><h2>Practical next steps</h2><ol><li>Check actual soil moisture and field drainage before preparing the seedbed. A dry-looking surface does not establish root-zone moisture.</li><li>Check borewell availability and water quality before deciding the sowing and irrigation plan.</li><li>Manage rice residue without burning. Confirm locally suitable methods and equipment.</li><li>Use a field-specific, representative soil test to discuss nutrient needs with the local agriculture department or Krishi Vigyan Kendra.</li><li>Confirm the wheat variety and sowing window with current local guidance. Do not treat the illustrative sowing period as a recommendation for every field.</li></ol></section>
<section class="farmer-card"><h2>Follow-up to record</h2><p>Before sowing, update field moisture, the confirmed sowing date, variety, irrigation availability and any verified laboratory result. After emergence, add a whole-field photo and a close-up if growth is uneven.</p><p><strong>Missing measurements:</strong> precise field coordinates, current field-level weather, actual soil moisture, verified soil chemistry, irrigation capacity and water quality. Values are not estimated from appearance.</p></section>
